]

For original double-box cell 0, native source-bound admission completed
*307 closed scalar charts*, retaining the full regulator density and the common
sufficient strip $-3/2 < Re(epsilon) < 1$. This produced *zero compiled kernels
and zero integral estimates*. The physical numerator was not admitted by this
scalar test. R5 took *4,122.9 s* and peaked at *9.96 GB own aggregate RSS*, with
one proof decode and fresh verification followed by caller-owned replay work.
It was a *debug preparation diagnostic*, not release CLI generation of a full
integral. The admitted objects ended with the process; saved receipts alone do
not recreate their source authority.

== Why catalogue sharing is not complete map coalescence

Exact prefix bookkeeping reduces 3,868,194 per-cell axis occurrences to
1,556,848 nodes, a factor of 2.485. Repeated root-bound uses similarly reduce
by a factor of 3.003. The 260 distinct global boundary polynomials and 45 global
support classes do not establish equality of selected branches, transformed
factors, maps or closed domains. Native compatibility merging retains the raw
cells and excluded seams as authority; it supplies no new cross-seam endpoint
atlas. No merged-count experiment was run before the work stopped.

A literal one-parent-per-cell implementation would represent 644,699 parents
before refinement. For scale only, *one chart per cell gives 644,699*, whereas
*307 per cell gives 197,922,593*. These are *illustrations, neither predictions
nor bounds* on the final chart count. No practical full-atlas runtime, memory,
artifact-size or sector-count forecast was established. The unfinished
closed-family continuation/compiler bridge and the remaining general boundary
cases prevent promoting the local result to a benchmark.

== Massive kite: a boundary is not a complete interval

The retained kite atlas has *9,840 original cells*. A genuine selected fourth
boundary was admitted over all *ten replacement charts of cell 0*, with
*40 strict checks at 128/256 bits*. This closes a boundary over three integration
directions. It does not close the fourth interval, whose width, denominators
and root-gap intersection with the upper face must join the complete
original $U$, $F$, numerator and Jacobian inventory. In particular, $F^(-1-2 epsilon)$ requires its own
common-strip and symbolic subtraction treatment.

A small native divided-difference probe established exact candidate width and
root identities, including higher-degree controls. Positivity on every closed
face, complete interval density, automatic coverage of all 9,840 cells and
full Laurent compilation/integration remain absent. Consequently the kite has
no complete no-contour benchmark here. The accepted local proofs and generic
implementation improvements remain useful without asserting a readily
available whole-integral result.#footnote[
  The claim-to-source index, immutable outcome identities and cancellation
  records are in `docs/reviews/no-contour-final-scope.md`.
]
